WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
I'm Mark Clement and I'll be doing the review. And Linda Cave is out at the table.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:13.000
She has a lot, she can answer any questions about logistics, about the course.

3
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:20.000
So she's best to ask on that. Usually if you have an ask question, I'll just refer you to her.

4
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:26.000
So just go to her first. She'll be here today and probably tomorrow and probably Sunday,

5
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:32.000
but I'm not an exacted sir on Sunday. She may or may not be here.

6
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.000
You should have in your possession a blue and yellow book.

7
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:46.000
If you don't and you didn't register, you need to go out and register now and get that done.

8
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:50.000
I'd like to thank Shannon and Derek for all their work getting us organized.

9
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.000
It's what you do. It's a lot of work. I appreciate it.

10
00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:06.000
Amazingly, it's just like two weeks time. I know professional event planners that can't pull off anything.

11
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:14.000
They have six months. You guys ought to go into event planning if you don't pass your board.

12
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:20.000
We're number three.

13
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.000
The lights, we don't know anything about the lights.

14
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.000
No, I tried getting them back on.

15
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:30.000
We have some function with the lights. Are they even trying or did they just say?

16
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.000
They come on and immediately go back on.

17
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.000
So they're not going to try and fix it?

18
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.000
Not this weekend.

19
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:42.000
So we're going to be... Now is that sunlight? Or is that ambient light?

20
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.000
Lighting is not light.

21
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:46.000
Is that not light up?

22
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:52.000
That's true. I was going to say if that's sunlight, when it goes down, it's going to be a little dark.

23
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:55.000
Hopefully it's just backlighting. Do you think that's what it is?

24
00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:56.000
Yeah, I think so.

25
00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:58.000
Okay, good. Well, that'll give us some lighting.

26
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:03.000
My eyes actually are... When I first came in here, they seem pretty dark in here, but it actually starts a lot lighter.

27
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:08.000
I guess I'm accommodating. So you'll have to take notes in this light.

28
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:13.000
I apologize for that, but that's just the way things happen sometimes.

29
00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:19.000
I think there's a little more light over here if you're having a hard time. That might be a good place to sit.

30
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:24.000
Can you see me? Okay, can you hear me?

31
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:31.000
Of course, if you didn't, you wouldn't answer anyhow. So I guess that's a stupid question to ask if you hear me or not.

32
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:36.000
But I don't usually use microphones because I don't usually need a microphone.

33
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:44.000
But it becomes a problem with you having to really attend to hear me, let me know, and I can use a microphone.

34
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:53.000
But I don't like to use it, and I don't usually have to, and I've lectured in auditoriums this size and bigger without microphones.

35
00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:58.000
And my voice usually lasts, so it should work.

36
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:04.000
Logistics, there are men and women's restrooms right out to the left.

37
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:08.000
For those of you that aren't from Mount Carmel, well, not anybody can use them.

38
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:13.000
I'm telling those of you that aren't from Mount Carmel where the restrooms are.

39
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:16.000
There are separate restrooms for Mount Carmel people.

40
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:20.000
But, I have to watch what I say, people take me literally.

41
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:24.000
Also, there are chips and salsa out there, I guess.

42
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:33.000
And then tomorrow, Jen's arranged for pizza to be brought in for lunchtime.

43
00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:36.000
And so you can plan on that.

44
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:42.000
And if you wanted to bring some extra little things to throw on the table, that'd be fine too.

45
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:43.000
Is that correct?

46
00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:44.000
Correct.

47
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:46.000
Correct.

48
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:48.000
Let's see.

49
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:52.000
Shannon, anything else I needed to tell them from your information?

50
00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:56.000
We are planning on a outlet, so if you want to get anybody a great baker,

51
00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:02.000
you might want to bring some things back and everything, as far as you want to go.

52
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:06.000
All right.

53
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:08.000
All right.

54
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:11.000
Let's see, what else?

55
00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:15.000
Is there any good wing planes around?

56
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:17.000
Here, do you want to get in?

57
00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:20.000
Maybe Sunday I could spring for wings.

58
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:22.000
Chris or Apple?

59
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:24.000
We'll see.

60
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:28.000
Let me know if there's something really good around here, you know what I mean?

61
00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:29.000
Okay.

62
00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:33.000
I don't know if I'll follow this that well.

63
00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.000
All right.

64
00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:37.000
Real quick introduction, then we'll get into it, because you're not here to learn about me.

65
00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:40.000
You're here to learn about the NCLEX RN.

66
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:44.000
So just so you know a little bit about me, some of you, most of you have never met me,

67
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:48.000
some of you have taken my class before and are here for a refresher.

68
00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:51.000
So that's cool.

69
00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:53.000
You also can do that.

70
00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:57.000
Let's say that some of you I know are from Wright State and some other places,

71
00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:00.000
and you may not graduate until June-ish.

72
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.000
Somebody may not graduate until June, whatever.

73
00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:08.000
If you keep your receipts, you can come to any other review I have at no cost,

74
00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:12.000
just show your receipt, and you can come.

75
00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:14.000
You know what I'm saying?

76
00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:23.000
So you can keep refreshing, you know, that kind of thing.

77
00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:24.000
All right.

78
00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:26.000
I am a registered nurse.

79
00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:31.000
I have been a nurse since 1973.

80
00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:35.000
So that means I've been a nurse for 36 years.

81
00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:36.000
36 years?

82
00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:37.000
That's all I am.

83
00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:38.000
36 years?

84
00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:39.000
How old is that year?

85
00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:41.000
So you're born the year I graduated.

86
00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:42.000
Okay.

87
00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:48.000
How many weren't born when I graduated from nurses?

88
00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:53.000
So I've been a nurse for 36 years, so it's been good to me.

89
00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:55.000
It's been a great profession.

90
00:05:55.000 --> 00:06:01.000
I will have to admit that in 1973 in Youngstown, Ohio,

91
00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:05.000
that was the steel mill closings.

92
00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:08.000
We were in a really big recession up there,

93
00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:13.000
and it took me three and a half months to find my first job as a nurse.

94
00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:17.000
But ever since then, I've had a minimum of two jobs going on at the same time.

95
00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:20.000
I've never been unemployed, never lost a job,

96
00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:22.000
always had at least two jobs going on.

97
00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:26.000
So it's a great – even in an economic downtime like it's now,

98
00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:28.000
your first job might be a little hard to find,

99
00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:30.000
but after that, it works pretty well.

100
00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:33.000
So it's been good to me, good to my family.

101
00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:34.000
I love it.

102
00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:35.000
I like to teach it.

103
00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:37.000
So hopefully you'll enjoy it.

104
00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:41.000
I try to stay current as well, you know, in practice, work in nursing,

105
00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:43.000
as well as teach in nursing.

106
00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:45.000
The last two years, I haven't been able to work in nursing

107
00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:49.000
because these reviews have just taken over my life.

108
00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:52.000
Every weekend, I'm somewhere.

109
00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:53.000
Which is – I'm not complaining.

110
00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:54.000
That's great.

111
00:06:54.000 --> 00:07:00.000
It's just I'm going to start saying no to some groups eventually.

112
00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.000
Let's see.

113
00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:05.000
I'm basically educated as a med surg nurse,

114
00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:10.000
but I also teach psych, and I worked in psych, so I know psych.

115
00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:16.000
I also do FARM, and I have graduate education in testing and measurement

116
00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:19.000
and cross-cultural nursing, tris-cultural nursing.

117
00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:22.000
Anybody ever hear of Madeline Leininger?

118
00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:24.000
Right state people should have heard of Leininger.

119
00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:25.000
Do you hear her?

120
00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:31.000
She was my advisor, so I'm an expert in cross-cultural and stuff like that.

121
00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:33.000
Let's see.

122
00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:38.000
I worked a lot at ICU, a lot at ER, step-down units.

123
00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:43.000
I usually have the idea that if something scares me, I go at it.

124
00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:47.000
I was scared of neuros, so I first started working in neuros.

125
00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:50.000
Then I was afraid of being able to read EKGs and rhythm strips,

126
00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:53.000
so I went into coronary care and learned that.

127
00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:55.000
Everything – you can learn anything.

128
00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:58.000
Just don't avoid – don't ever think you can't learn something

129
00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:00.000
or that something's too hard for you.

130
00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:04.000
Just go at it, and you'll find that it's not a big deal.

131
00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:05.000
Let's see what else.

132
00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:08.000
I have a wife with four daughters.

133
00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:11.000
Yeah.

134
00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:13.000
I better shut myself off.

135
00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:14.000
It's Friday.

136
00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:15.000
They're teenagers.

137
00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:18.000
They're going to be wanting money.

138
00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:19.000
I better shut up.

139
00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:24.000
Well, there.

140
00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:28.000
Now I want to –

141
00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:31.000
but they always – four daughters, I think.

142
00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:38.000
They're 22, 21, 20, and 12.

143
00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:41.000
So we're just getting out of those teenagers.

144
00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:44.000
But they still do – I had my 20-year-old.

145
00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:48.000
I got a phone call an hour ago from my insurance company.

146
00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:54.000
He said, we have a claim on the state of New York from your daughter

147
00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:57.000
who spun out and hit a mile marker.

148
00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:01.000
And the state of New York has filed a claim on your insurance company

149
00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:04.000
to replace the mile marker.

150
00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:07.000
$1,200.

151
00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:10.000
It's like a mile marker.

152
00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:11.000
Geez.

153
00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:12.000
Give me a break.

154
00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:13.000
That's not what happened with the –

155
00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:16.000
you imagine 20-year-old's insurance is going to go up, too.

156
00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:21.000
But anyhow – okay, I know you care.

157
00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:23.000
Let's see what else.

158
00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:24.000
That's basically it.

159
00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:27.000
I've taught at Cedarwood University for 27 years.

160
00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:30.000
I've taught at nursing there for – this is my 27th year.

161
00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:32.000
And I stick with – I'm sticking them out.

162
00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:35.000
I'm just one town, one job, one wife.

163
00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:37.000
I just sort of stick with them.

164
00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:39.000
I don't like change.

165
00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:43.000
If I could have gotten rid of the kids, I would have been –

166
00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:45.000
I had stuck with them forever.

167
00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:46.000
No, I'm just kidding.

168
00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:50.000
But that's where I am.

169
00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:52.000
So we'll go around the room.

170
00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:54.000
You've got to tell them your name and something.

171
00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:55.000
Nobody else knows about you.

172
00:09:55.000 --> 00:09:57.000
No, we're not going to do that.

173
00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:58.000
You're just going, oh.

174
00:09:58.000 --> 00:09:59.000
Oh, thank goodness.

175
00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:01.000
There's a safe place to do that.

176
00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:02.000
Go to the bathroom, introduce yourself.

177
00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:04.000
We're not doing that, okay?

178
00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:05.000
Forget it.

179
00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:08.000
Okay, what the goal is here, while we're together,

180
00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:10.000
I have three objectives, three objectives.

181
00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:12.000
First, I want you to acquire some knowledge.

182
00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:14.000
I want you to learn something.

183
00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:17.000
I want you to go out that door and go, boy, I learned something today.

184
00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:20.000
Because I don't assume that everybody knows everything.

185
00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:24.000
And it doesn't hurt to learn some new information every once in a while.

186
00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:28.000
And I know enough about nursing to know that every graduate doesn't know

187
00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:31.000
everything they could have done for this board.

188
00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:34.000
There's some information that you need to know for boards that you do not know.

189
00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:36.000
Now, it's different for everybody.

190
00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:37.000
Not everybody has that same deficit.

191
00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:41.000
You have one that someone else doesn't have, and vice versa.

192
00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:43.000
But I want you to acquire some knowledge.

193
00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:46.000
Some reviews, you don't learn anything.

194
00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:49.000
All they do is they just do multiple choice questions.

195
00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:52.000
And you're learning how to apply it.

196
00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:54.000
Well, that assumes you know it.

197
00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:56.000
And you can't apply what you don't know.

198
00:10:56.000 --> 00:11:02.000
So a lot of good it does to sit there and try to apply something if you don't know what they're talking about in the first place.

199
00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:04.000
To me, that's a waste of time.

200
00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:12.000
And I've worked in nursing long enough education to know that students don't know everything that they probably should know.

201
00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:14.000
They've forgotten some stuff.

202
00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:19.000
So we're going to try to spit up your knowledge, and I want you to acquire some knowledge.

203
00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:21.000
Secondly, I want you to gain confidence.

204
00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:24.000
I want you to, when you walk out of here, go, you know, I can do this.

205
00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:26.000
This is something that I can do.

206
00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:30.000
Because I think one of the biggest fears that people have is that they're going to fail.

207
00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:38.000
Because somehow, some way, they're the one person in the universe that this is just beyond their capabilities.

208
00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:41.000
So everybody else will be able to do it.

209
00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:43.000
But they will not.

210
00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:48.000
For some inexplicable reason, it's beyond their capabilities.

211
00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:51.000
And I got through school, and I got through that.

212
00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:55.000
But when I go to the boards, it's just going to get me.

213
00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:58.000
Those standardized tests are just going to get me.

214
00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:00.000
And you don't want to go there.

215
00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:10.000
So I want you to have confidence that you've done everything that you humanly possibly could have done when you sit down to take that board.

216
00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:17.000
Because the worst-case scenario is you go to boards, and you know some of your friends, some of your classmates don't do any reviewing for boards.

217
00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:19.000
They just go take it.

218
00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:21.000
Well, that's okay.

219
00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:30.000
But can you imagine yourself, you sit down for boards, and you sign your name in, and you click go, and it brings up the first question,

220
00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:36.000
and suddenly you realize you haven't done anything to prepare for this?

221
00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:40.000
Can you imagine how you would feel if you had not done a thing to prepare?

222
00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:45.000
Your friends that aren't taking review classes might be confident now.

223
00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:49.000
I'd like to talk to them when that first question pops up and say,

224
00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:52.000
hey, would you think you might want to do something?

225
00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:54.000
But then it's too late.

226
00:12:54.000 --> 00:13:01.000
So when you sit down and start taking that test, I want you to realize that you've done everything you possibly could have done.

227
00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:09.000
And you did what is above and beyond reasonability, and you can have that confidence that you've done your part.

228
00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:15.000
Secondly, I want you to become exam proficient, meaning I want you to know how to take tests,

229
00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:24.000
because earlier I just said you can't apply what you don't know, but you have to be able to apply what you do know.

230
00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:31.000
So it takes three things to pass boards, knowledge, confidence, and skill in taking exams.

231
00:13:31.000 --> 00:13:36.000
So this review is designed to address all three of those points.

232
00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:41.000
Have you acquired some knowledge, gained some confidence, and gained some exam proficiency?

233
00:13:41.000 --> 00:13:47.000
When you walk out of here on Sunday, I want you to think, you know, I know how to do those select all the applies.

234
00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:50.000
I know how to do those click and drags.

235
00:13:50.000 --> 00:14:03.000
I know how to do prioritization questions, delegation questions, staff management questions, best questions, first questions, all those kinds of different formats.

236
00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:07.000
I want you to be able to do point and click in all of those.

237
00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:09.000
So we'll be talking about that.

238
00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:16.000
I really believe that any review, there are two types of reviews out there, and if you've had reviews, you know this.

239
00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:21.000
